{
  "header": [
    "UNALLOCATED — an entity whose content_hash names a format code the peer does not support is refused 400 unsupported_content_hash_format.",
    "",
    "Batch 3. A SHALL with a pinned code (§4.7 row 5), a named put-admission route (§6.3), and no §9 row.",
    "§4.7 itself says this is NOT a handshake obligation, so it does not ride on the §4.7 floor row."
  ],
  "id_status": "unallocated",
  "under": "",
  "title": "A system/tree:put whose entity content_hash carries an unallocated format code is refused 400 unsupported_content_hash_format, not treated as a mismatch, a miss or a malformed entity",
  "spec": "ENTITY-CORE-PROTOCOL §1.2 (Content-hash format-code interpretation)",
  "spec_also": [
    "§4.7 row 5 and its 'Row 5's surface is ingest, not the handshake' paragraph",
    "§6.3 put admission step 1 (the unsupported-format case routed to row 5)",
    "ENTITY-CBOR-ENCODING §4.7 (construction-vs-verification asymmetry: the verification path MUST reject)"
  ],
  "snapshot": "entity-core-protocol/v0.8.2.21",
  "level": "MUST",
  "profile": [
    "core"
  ],
  "surface": "wire",
  "status": "draft",
  "routed": "docs/status/FINDINGS-FROM-AUTHORING.md F20, F23",
  "reading": [
    "§1.2: \"An implementation receiving a `content_hash` whose format code it does not support SHALL return",
    "`unsupported_content_hash_format` (per §4.7) rather than silently failing or treating an unknown-format",
    "hash as a content miss.\" SHALL is MUST (RFC 2119; ENTITY-CBOR-ENCODING §1.4 adopts it). §4.7 pins the status: 400.",
    "§6.3 names the put route explicitly: \"A `content_hash` that is well-formed but names a format code the",
    "peer does not support is the separate §1.2 ingest-dispatch case and is refused 400",
    "`unsupported_content_hash_format` (§4.7 row 5), not this row [invalid_request].\" ENTITY-CBOR-ENCODING",
    "§4.7: \"The `content_hash` verification path (decoder) MUST reject any unsupported or unallocated",
    "`format_code` with `unsupported_content_hash_format`\".",
    "",
    "WHY PUT AND NOT THE HELLO. §4.7 row 5 is \"reachable on any frame carrying an unallocated format code\",",
    "so a hello is a legal surface too. But a hello's root is a wire/identity-surface entity that §4.5a item",
    "2 forbids the SENDER to author under a non-negotiated format, and whether a responder answers that as a",
    "§1.2 dispatch or as a sequence/validity failure is not stated. Put is the one surface where the text",
    "names this code for this input; the hello is witnessed.",
    "",
    "WHICH CODE. 0x7E: in §1.2's \"0x0A–0xEF Reserved (future)\" range, so no peer can support it conformantly",
    "today, and outside the range where ECF §4.3's registry disagrees with core's (F20). It is single-byte,",
    "so a peer that reads the format code as an OCTET (ECF §4.5) and one that reads a varint (§1.2) see the",
    "same code — this probe does not depend on F20.",
    "",
    "\"WELL-FORMED\" IS THE CONDITION, SO THE HASH IS 33 BYTES. §6.3 routes to row 5 only a hash that is",
    "otherwise well-formed. The digest length of an unallocated code is not defined, so \"matches its format",
    "code\" cannot be evaluated for it; a 33-byte hash is the length a peer that assumes 32-byte digests",
    "would accept, which makes any other answer attributable to the code, not the length."
  ],
  "preconditions": {
    "grants": [
      "system/tree: put"
    ],
    "identities": [
      "caller"
    ],
    "handlers": [],
    "posture_axes": [
      "seed_policy",
      "content_hash_format"
    ],
    "note": "without a put grant the put member is UNMEASURABLE (a 403), reported so; the hello member still runs as a witness."
  },
  "arm": [
    {
      "name": "put-unsupported-format",
      "kind": "conformant",
      "given": "an authenticated connection; system/tree:put of {type: \"test/v1\", data: {\"x\": 1}, content_hash: 0x7E || SHA-256(ECF({type, data}))}",
      "why": "§1.2's SHALL at the surface §6.3 routes to it.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "connect"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "put_7e",
          "uri": "system/tree",
          "operation": "put"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "status",
          "capture": "put_7e",
          "equals": 400
        },
        {
          "kind": "error_code",
          "capture": "put_7e",
          "equals": "unsupported_content_hash_format",
          "why": "NOT hash_mismatch (the peer recomputed under an assumed algorithm), NOT invalid_request (the peer judged length against an assumed digest size), NOT a 404-style miss."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "hello-unsupported-format",
      "kind": "conformant",
      "given": "a fresh connection; a hello whose root content_hash is 0x7E || the SHA-256 digest",
      "why": "WITNESS-ONLY: row 5 is reachable here, but the responder's precedence against §4.5a is unstated.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "send_hello",
          "capture": "hello_7e",
          "corrupt": "root.content_hash format byte 0x00 → 0x7E"
        }
      ],
      "witness": [
        {
          "field": "status_and_code",
          "why": "cohort data."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "control",
      "kind": "negative-control",
      "given": "the same put with format byte 0x00 and the same digest",
      "why": "MANDATORY ANTI-VACUITY. Same digest, same entity, one byte different. A 200 here and the coded refusal in the probe can only be about the format code. Without it, a peer refusing every put from this suite passes.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "put_00",
          "uri": "system/tree",
          "operation": "put"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "status",
          "capture": "put_00",
          "equals": 200
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "non-conformant-witness",
      "kind": "non-conformant",
      "given": "a peer that ignores the format byte and hashes everything with SHA-256",
      "expect": "the 0x7E put is ACCEPTED (the digest matches), and the stored entity is addressed under a hash that names an algorithm nobody computed",
      "why": "the silent failure §1.2's 'rather than silently failing' names — and the reason the probe's digest is the correct SHA-256: it is the input that exposes a format-blind peer."
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
    "peer_attributable": true,
    "oracle_check": "encoding/hash_algorithm_byte",
    "oracle_note": "provenance only — its name suggests the format byte; it cites ENTITY-CBOR-ENCODING §4.3 (the registry that disagrees with core, F20)."
  }
}
